  "worked_solution": "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/7873579bb5dd1976330ec24f13b30dd384f916cf23bbf48aac4b2a522ee9548a.png)\n\nWe're given that $cos\u2061\\theta =-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin\u2061\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ sin\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos\u2061\\theta =-\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $sin\u2061\\theta $ is negative in the third quadrant. So, $sin\u2061\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan\u2061\\theta =\\frac{sin\u2061\\theta }{cos\u2061\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan\u2061\\theta =\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
